  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q21-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "21",
    "prompt": "Let $a,c>0$, $b>0$ and $b\\ne1$. In part (b), also assume $a\\ne1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $a^{\\log_b(c)}=c^{\\log_b(a)}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^{\\log_b(c)}=c^{\\log_b(a)}$.\n\nTaking logarithms to base $b$ gives the same product on both sides; logarithms are one-to-one.",
          "worked": "Let $x=a^{\\log_b(c)}$ and $y=c^{\\log_b(a)}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_b x&=\\log_b\\left(a^{\\log_b(c)}\\right)\\\\\\log_b x&=\\log_b(c)\\log_b(a)\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_b y&=\\log_b\\left(c^{\\log_b(a)}\\right)\\\\\\log_b y&=\\log_b(a)\\log_b(c)\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\log_b x=\\log_b y$. Since logarithms are one-to-one, $x=y$, so $a^{\\log_b(c)}=c^{\\log_b(a)}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence show that $\\log_b(a)\\cdot\\log_a(c)=\\log_b(c)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\log_b(a)\\log_a(c)=\\log_b(c)$.\n\nTake logarithms to base $a$ of the identity in part (a) and use the power law.",
          "worked": "Taking logarithms to base $a$ of the identity in part (a):\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_a\\left(a^{\\log_b(c)}\\right)&=\\log_a\\left(c^{\\log_b(a)}\\right)\\\\\\log_b(c)\\log_a(a)&=\\log_b(a)\\log_a(c)\\\\\\log_b(c)&=\\log_b(a)\\log_a(c)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      }
    ]
  },
